---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Code from '../../../components/Code.astro';
import Notice from '../../../components/Notice.astro';
const toc = [{ id: 'theme', text: 'Create the theme' }, { id: 'tailwind', text: 'MUI next to Tailwind' }, { id: 'gaps', text: 'Known gaps' }];
const themeCode = `import { ThemeProvider } from '@mui/material/styles';
import CssBaseline from '@mui/material/CssBaseline';
import { createFlintTheme } from '@innerspark/flint/mui';

const theme = createFlintTheme({ mode: 'light', density: 'comfortable' });

export default function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <CssBaseline />
      {/* your app */}
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
}`;
const layerCode = `import { StyledEngineProvider } from '@mui/material/styles';
import GlobalStyles from '@mui/material/GlobalStyles';

<StyledEngineProvider enableCssLayer>
  <GlobalStyles styles="@layer theme, base, mui, components, utilities;" />
  {/* ThemeProvider and the app */}
</StyledEngineProvider>`;
---
<Docs illo="theme" title="MUI" crumb="Get started" lead="A ready MUI theme built from Flint: palette, type, spacing, breakpoints, elevation and a set of component overrides, in light and dark." toc={toc}>
  <h2 id="theme">Create the theme</h2>
  <p><code>@mui/material</code> is an optional peer dependency. It is only needed if you import this entry.</p>
  <Code label="React" code={themeCode} />
  <p>Pass <code>mode: 'dark'</code> for dark and <code>density: 'compact'</code> for compact. <code>flintThemeOptions(mode, density)</code> returns the raw options if you build the theme yourself.</p>

  <h2 id="tailwind">MUI next to Tailwind</h2>
  <p>MUI's baseline reset is not in a cascade layer, so on a page that also uses Tailwind it overrides every Tailwind utility. Put MUI in its own layer, below Tailwind's utilities.</p>
  <Code label="React" code={layerCode} />

  <h2 id="gaps">Known gaps</h2>
  <Notice tone="warning" title="What the MUI theme can't change">
    <p>The theme now reads Flint's component tokens for buttons, inputs, checkboxes, radios, switches, icons, tabs and menu items (ASDS-44). What's left is MUI's own behavior: a round halo on checkbox and radio focus, a <code>loading</code> button that hides its label, a TextField label that floats inside the input, an Alert that is <code>role="alert"</code> by default, and a Snackbar that shows one message at a time. Each component's Code tab says how to work around it, and the <a href="/workbench/">workbench</a> shows MUI next to the other implementations.</p>
  </Notice>
</Docs>
